{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"major-specified-bream","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The observation\n\n`caused_by` auto-derivation falls back to best-effort `git blame` over a ticket's impacted paths\nwhen no `--caused-by` is supplied (`docs/clone-guidance.md`). Across **eight** attributions\nestablished under `/rebar-debug` in a single session (2026-08-20), each with a proven mechanism and\na deliberate blame check, that fallback would have produced a **wrong or empty** answer in roughly\nsix of eight cases.\n\nThis is not an argument that the fallback is badly implemented. It is an argument that the\n**dominant defect shape in this codebase is one blame cannot see**.\n\n## The evidence\n\n| bug | attributed origin | what blame would have named |\n|---|---|---|\n| `bediasite-bilineate-stoat` | `cec6-0a0e-38a9-4234` | correct — omission at origin, single commit |\n| `perkish-balsamic-pupfish` | `fc20-2df1-426e-42ab` | the GATE's author, whose code was correct when written. `fc20` extended administrative dispositions to task/story/epic, modified `close_precheck.py` and `transition_close.py`, and **never touched `gates.py`** |\n| `irongrey-chubby-oxpecker` | `789c-1251-fc21-4cf6` | a consumer written against a producer contract its own plan asserted but never verified |\n| `moist-short-lionfish` | `c1de-d6a0-6cef-4135` | `e1f4`, which WROTE `pending_enrichment` — correctly, for drain-time enumeration. `c1de` is the change that put it on the WRITE path under a 20 ms budget it declared itself |\n| `electronic-deducible-maggot` | `8d36-a15a-fcbd-4ebd` | a **June file-move carrier** in `test_cli.py` predating the transition entirely |\n| `steep-tired-whimbrel` | `c7c5-12ea-ad68-4690` | `051b82836bf`, a **carrier** (an extraction of identical logic); found by walking back with `git log -S \"could not read rebar config\"` |\n| `itchy-arbored-peccary` | `b73c-…` + `615c-…` (two) | nothing usable — both are **omissions**: a promise made in ADR 0005 D5 that no ticket's ACs ever obliged anyone to implement |\n| `ruling-magnific-bats` | `555e-3efa-4ad6-4169` | correct — here the cause site genuinely IS the fix site, established by the date test rather than assumed |\n| `nonodorous-snotty-seaurchin` | *unattributable* | the guard has been wrong since `tests/conftest.py`'s first commit, which predates the `rebar-ticket:` convention |\n\n## Why blame fails, stated precisely\n\nThree distinct mechanisms, all common here:\n\n1. **Unmet obligation.** A change creates a new contract — a field, a signature, an invariant, a\n   config key, a changed default — and discharges it on some consumers but not all. The consumer\n   that was missed may be untouched for months and still be where the failure surfaces and where\n   the fix lands. Blame names whoever last edited a line that was correct when written. This was\n   the single most common shape.\n2. **Carrier commits.** A reformat, rename, file move or mechanical extraction sits on top of the\n   real origin. `git blame -w -C -M` helps but does not always suffice; twice today the origin was\n   only reachable by `git log -S<symbol>` on the PRODUCER side.\n3. **Omission.** Nothing ever broke, so no commit made it wrong. An ADR promised a byte-total\n   backstop and no ticket's acceptance criteria ever obliged its implementation. There is no\n   commit for blame to find, by construction.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe `caused_by` edge exists to feed `rebar metrics`' escape-rate lenses and the epic-close\n`caused_by` floor. A **wrong** edge is worse than an absent one: it silently charges an escaped\ndefect to a ticket whose reviewers could not plausibly have caught it, and it is indistinguishable\nfrom a considered attribution on read. The fallback fails CLOSED on a sub-majority or a partial\nread, which is the right instinct — but the cases above are ones where blame returns a confident\nmajority for the wrong file.\n\nSo the metric's accuracy depends far more on agents supplying `--caused-by` explicitly than the\nexistence of a fallback suggests. That is worth knowing before anyone reads escape-rate numbers as\nif they were measured rather than largely inferred.\n\n## What this ticket should determine\n\n- **Measure it, do not take this sample as the answer.** Nine attributions from one session is a\n  suggestive sample, not a rate. Sample closed bugs carrying a `caused_by` edge, re-derive the\n  origin independently, and quantify how often the recorded edge is the blame-derived guess versus\n  a supplied proof — and how often the guess is wrong.\n- **Decide whether a wrong edge should be distinguishable from a proven one on read.** Today they\n  are not. A `derived: blame` versus `derived: explicit` marker on the edge would let the metric\n  weight them differently, or exclude guesses entirely. That is a small schema question with a\n  large effect on whether the numbers mean anything.\n- **Consider whether the fallback should run at all** when the mechanism is an omission or an\n  unmet obligation — cases it structurally cannot get right. It may be better to record nothing\n  and say so than to record a plausible wrong answer.\n- **Consider a review-time criterion** for the dominant shape: a change that introduces a new\n  contract should be asked whether every consumer of that contract was updated. That is the\n  producer-side question, and it is the one nobody is currently asked.\n\n## Relationship to sibling tickets\n\n- `marshy-chummy-coot` (`2399-5e49-6b28-4d59`) asks how a silently-inert guard escaped review and\n  tests. This ticket asks a different question — not how a defect escaped DETECTION, but why the\n  record of where it CAME FROM is unreliable. Related, not duplicate.\n- `ruling-magnific-bats` (`10d0-c26c-bdbb-4b1f`) is the mechanical defect in the same area: the\n  close's `--caused-by` write is not idempotent. Its second comment argues the guidance to always\n  pass the flag must stand precisely BECAUSE the blame fallback is unreliable — which is the claim\n  this ticket exists to test rather than assume.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The rate is measured, not estimated: a sample of closed bugs with `caused_by` edges is\n      re-derived independently, and the proportion of blame-derived edges that name a defensibly\n      wrong origin is reported with the sample size and method.\n- [ ] A decision is recorded on whether a blame-derived edge should be distinguishable from an\n      explicitly supplied one on read, with the reasoning.\n- [ ] A decision is recorded on whether the fallback should decline to attribute for the shapes it\n      cannot get right, rather than guessing.\n- [ ] Any proposed review-time criterion or schema change is filed as its own ticket rather than\n      implemented here.","id":"50e3-6d2a-0e7f-4e92","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Measure whether caused_by blame auto-derivation is reliable: 8 session attributions suggest it is usually wrong"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787270211033922001,"uuid":"dc1ca79b-4a56-476b-982e-72b599587402"}